  "root_cause": "On input buffer growth/OOM error paths, xmlBufSetInputBaseCur reset parser input pointers (base/cur/end) to a 1-byte global empty string literal (\"\"). Parsing could continue and compute a positive name length, then call xmlDictLookup. xmlDictComputeFastKey hashes by reading up to 10 bytes (e.g., name[9]) based only on the provided length, causing a 1-byte (or more) out-of-bounds read past the empty sentinel into adjacent globals.",
  "fix_approach": "Harden the error sentinel and the NCName complex lookup boundary: (1) replace the 1-byte empty-string literal used as the error sentinel with a padded static NUL buffer, and set input->end accordingly, so accidental small reads can’t go past the object; (2) add a bounds/consistency check in xmlParseNCNameComplex before calling xmlDictLookup to ensure the (base+startPosition,len) span lies within the current input buffer. This prevents passing impossible pointer/length pairs to dict hashing during error handling.",
